        "completedSourceTexts": [
          {
            "textId": "text:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:completed:1",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:1",
            "text": "For many kids, setting a big goal and achieving it can feel like climbing a slippery hill. But SMART goals can give them the tools to conquer it with confidence. Teaching children to create goals that are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound builds a can-do attitude and helps them face obstacles in life."
          },
          {
            "textId": "text:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:completed:2",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:2",
            "text": "SMART goals deconstruct larger goals into smaller, actionable steps. They remove guesswork and make goals easier to track. Goals should be specific and detailed. For example, an unclear goal like “do better at math” can be reframed as “practice multiplication tables for 10 minutes after dinner every day”."
          },
          {
            "textId": "text:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:completed:3",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:3",
            "text": "When you set SMART goals with kids, the key is to keep them collaborative and simple. First, avoid making goals too big or setting too many of them. They can make kids feel overwhelmed, so it’s better to focus on one simple goal at a time until it becomes a habit."
          },
          {
            "textId": "text:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:completed:4",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:4",
            "text": "It’s also important to avoid common mistakes. One of them is paying no attention to process. Praising only the results instead of the effort will make kids discouraged when they face setbacks. What’s more, don’t set goals for your kids – let them take ownership of their own goals."
          },
          {
            "textId": "text:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:completed:5",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-shandong-taian-2026-04-second-round-test:seven-choices:5",
            "text": "Setting the goal is just the first step; sticking to it is more important. There are several tips to help kids stick to their SMART goals. Regular check-ins, celebrating small progress and adjusting goals when needed can all help kids stay on track with their SMART goals. In the end, SMART goals are not just about achieving something, but about building life skills like time management and self-discipline for kids."
          }
        ],
